WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.220
Processes run in a current working directory.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:11.640
If a script assumes the wrong directory, the invoked tool may read, write, scan, or generate files in the wrong location.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.580
That can break builds, confuse operators, or affect data the script never intended to touch.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.440
Safe invocation makes directory assumptions explicit.

5
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:29.460
Uncontrolled redirection can overwrite output, hide errors, merge streams, or place sensitive data into logs.

6
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.580
Input and output paths should be validated before invocation.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:42.960
A script should know whether a path must exist, whether it must be a file or directory, whether it should be relative or absolute, and which base locations are acceptable.

8
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:48.740
When output is involved, the script should avoid accidental overwrites and handle existing files deliberately.

9
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.620
Redirection deserves care.

10
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.880
It can overwrite output, hide errors, merge streams, or place sensitive data into logs.

11
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.860
Redirection is not merely formatting.

12
00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.880
It changes how a process communicates and where evidence goes.

13
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.700
A safe script redirects only when the destination and purpose are clear.

14
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.460
Standard input, standard output, and standard error should be handled intentionally.

15
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.320
Some tools read from standard input when no file is provided.

16
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.300
Some write machine-readable data to standard output and human-readable errors to standard error.

17
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.240
Keeping those streams separate where useful makes automation easier to parse and easier to troubleshoot.

18
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:34.120
Logging should help operators without exposing secrets or sensitive file contents.

19
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:41.820
Capture high-level actions, paths at an appropriate sensitivity level, exit results, and clear failure messages.

20
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:50.480
Avoid dumping full output when it may contain tokens, private data, or configuration details that do not belong in routine logs.

21
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:57.940
Review confirms the intended standard-input, standard-output, standard-error, and redirection destinations and the evidence each produces.
